<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Sorbs</h1><p class="st-meta">631 CE – 932 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">West Slavic tribes between the Saale and the Bóbr whose descendants, the Sorbs of Lusatia, are still a recognised minority in Germany.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Slavic groups settled the lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> in the 6th and 7th centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a> records that in 631 the Sorb prince Dervan left Frankish allegiance and joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samos-empire">Samo's Empire</a>. Their territory included the regions later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daleminzia">Daleminzia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Milzenland</a> (Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>) and Lusatia.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Sorbs were organised in small tribal territories, each centred on fortified strongholds (gords) such as those at Tornow and Raddusch, with no lasting central ruler. They farmed, raised livestock and worshipped Slavic gods. Frankish sources record frequent wars and tribute payments, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-sorabicus">Limes Sorabicus</a> marked the boundary with them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia's</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> took the Daleminzian stronghold of Gana in 929 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. By 932 the Sorbian tribes were tributary, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marca-geronis">Marca Geronis</a> and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-meissen">Margraviate of Meissen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> absorbed them. Sorbs survive today in Lusatia, keeping their language.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sorbs.jpg" alt="Sorbs" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Mysid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sorbs.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>631 CE – 932 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dervan, Miliduch</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Slavic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sorbian (West Slavic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sorbian tribes, Surbi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=782&amp;focus=sorbs" aria-label="Sorbs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4877 411 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sorbs lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5199 691h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5197 667h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5197 667h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Germany. <a href="/atlas?year=782&amp;focus=sorbs">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

